    "created_at": "2017-08-16T17:53:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-08-16T17:53:56Z",
    "body": "It would take a bit of time to try it out, so I'll do so if the interface is actually changed or if it's agreed that it will be changed based on the results of my experiment.  Basically right now the signature is\r\n\r\n```c++\r\n  void NamedGetter(JSContext* cx, const nsAString& aName, bool& aFound,\r\n                   JS::MutableHandle<JSObject*> aRetval,\r\n                   mozilla::ErrorResult& rv);\r\n```\r\n\r\nand returning the value involves something like\r\n\r\n```c++\r\n  // Suppose collection is our HTMLCollection\r\n  JS::Rooted<JS::Value> val(cx);\r\n  if (!dom::WrapObject(aCx, collection, nullptr, &val)) {\r\n    aRv.Throw(NS_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY);\r\n    aFound = false;\r\n    aRetval.set(nullptr);\r\n    return;\r\n  }\r\n  aFound = true;\r\n  aRetval.set(&val.toObject);\r\n  return;\r\n```\r\nwhereas I think with unions it might be something like\r\n```c++\r\n  void NamedGetter(const nsAString& aName, bool& aFound,\r\n                   Nullable<OwningHTMLCollectionOrHTMLElementOrWindowProxy>& aReturn,\r\n                   mozilla::ErrorResult& rv);\r\n```\r\nand returning would be something like\r\n```c++\r\n  aReturn.AsValue().AsHTMLCollection() = collection;\r\n  aFound = true;\r\n  return;\r\n```\r\nOn the other hand, if we spelled out every type, the union class name would be ridiculously long and would require tons of cases to return a value, probably also necessitating runtime type checking that shouldn't be necessary.\r\n\r\nI'm not sure this should directly have any bearing on what's in the spec, since I don't think there's any reason in principle why the object case couldn't look more or less like the union case, but it's an example of how more generic types can wind up being more complicated to deal with.  Since our binding code has to allow for the possibility of absolutely any object being returned, it needs to expose the callee to the full complexity of a JSObject instead of wrapping it up in nice types, in case the callee wants to return something crazy.  If it knows that the callee only wants to return one of three types, it could generate much nicer code.\r\n\r\nA more specific return type also would automatically enforce that we return the correct types using C++'s built-in type safety.  Currently, in fact, we return a NodeList rather than an HTMLCollection, and the binding code does nothing to stop that.  If it was properly typed, we couldn't.  (This argues in favor of fully spelling it out, but I think that's just too long and awkward in practice.)",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
